  "reason": "The records point toward the result but do not fully anticipate it. R1 provides the strongest intervention precedent: annealing at 600 °C in a closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloy produces decomposition and hardening. R7 links thermal treatment to gamma-prime/L12-type phase evolution in a related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloy, while R8 identifies FCC/B2 transition-layer interfaces as sites of strain localization and cracking in a closely related eutectic alloy. R2, R4, R6, R10, R14, and R19 add partial links involving ordering, modulus changes, plastic instability, thermal phase instability, and cross-slip locking. However, none combines intermediate-temperature aging with maximized L12 order, jagged FCC/B2 boundaries, elevated APBE, planar slip, and subsequent recovery through reduced ordering and ductile boundary zones. An expert could infer that aging might produce a non-monotonic mechanical response, but the specific ductile-brittle-ductile mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
